Service Catalog Graph-Based Design Platform

Design a modular application that models an organization’s ServiceNow Service Catalog as a graph rather than as a collection of forms or database tables.

Vision

The application is not a ServiceNow development tool. Instead, it is a visual design environment where business analysts, product owners, and service designers can understand the current state of the Service Catalog, propose changes, and generate implementation-ready user stories for a ServiceNow development team.

ServiceNow remains the system of record, while this application becomes the system of design.

Core Concept

The application imports the existing ServiceNow Service Catalog and converts it into a directed graph.

The graph represents the complete catalog structure and relationships, including:

* Catalogs
* Categories
* Catalog Items
* Variable Sets
* Variables
* Choices
* UI Policies (future)
* Approvals (future)
* Workflows (future)
* Dependencies between components

Nodes represent entities.

Edges represent relationships such as:

* contains
* belongs_to
* uses
* depends_on
* reveals
* requires
* references

The graph is the canonical representation of the catalog inside the application.

User Experience

Users interact with the graph through a visual drag-and-drop interface.

They should be able to:

* Browse the existing catalog
* Navigate relationships
* Expand and collapse branches
* Search for existing components
* Reuse existing components
* Create new components
* Modify existing components
* Move components to different parents
* Rename components
* Remove components
* Replace relationships
* Clone existing structures
* Compare alternative designs before committing changes

The interface should make the catalog feel like a living design model rather than a collection of forms.

Editing Operations

All user actions are represented as graph operations.

Examples include:

Create:

* Add Catalog
* Add Category
* Add Catalog Item
* Add Variable
* Add Variable Set
* Add Choice

Modify:

* Rename nodes
* Edit properties
* Change parent relationships
* Attach or detach Variable Sets
* Change variable order
* Update choice values
* Modify dependencies

Delete:

* Remove variables
* Remove variable sets
* Remove catalog items
* Remove categories
* Remove relationships
* Mark components for retirement

Reuse:

* Reuse Variable Sets
* Reuse Variables
* Clone Catalog Items
* Copy existing structures

Every change should be tracked as a graph transformation.

Graph Versioning

The imported ServiceNow catalog becomes the baseline graph.

User changes create a working graph.

The application continuously computes the difference between:

Current Graph
vs
Proposed Graph

The resulting graph diff becomes the source for implementation artifacts.

Change Analysis

The application should clearly identify:

New components

Modified components

Removed components

Moved components

Reused components

Relationship changes

Dependency changes

Nothing should be inferred; every change should be explicitly represented.

Outputs

The primary output is not deployment.

The primary output is documentation for implementation teams.

Outputs include:

* User Stories
* Acceptance Criteria
* Change Summary
* Impact Summary
* Visual Graph Diff
* Architecture Diagrams
* Exportable Graph JSON

User Story Generation

Every graph operation maps to one or more implementation-ready user stories.

Examples:

Adding a Catalog Item generates a story to create the new item.

Renaming a Variable generates a story describing the update.

Removing a Variable Set generates a story describing its retirement and any affected catalog items.

Moving a Catalog Item between categories generates a story describing the structural change.

The generated stories should be deterministic and based entirely on graph differences rather than AI inference.

Architecture

The system should be modular and composed of:

1. ServiceNow Catalog Extractor
2. Graph Builder
3. Graph Repository
4. Visual Graph Editor
5. Graph Diff Engine
6. Change Analyzer
7. User Story Generator
8. Documentation Exporter

Each module should be independently replaceable.

Design Principles

* The graph is the single source of truth.
* Every edit is represented as a graph transformation.
* Existing structures should be reusable rather than duplicated.
* The application should support additions, modifications, moves, reuse, and removals equally well.
* Implementation is intentionally outside the scope of the application.
* The focus is on planning, visualization, governance, and producing implementation-ready change requests for the ServiceNow development team.
